---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for L-1 Visa Holders | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for L-1 visa holders explains why some intracompany transferees evaluate a self-sponsored green card instead of relying only on continued L-1 eligibility.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-l1`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for L-1 Visa Holders';
const subhead =
  'Why some L-1 professionals evaluate EB-5 as a permanent alternative to continued employer transfer status.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'H-1B to Green Card via EB-5',
    url: '/pathways/h1b-to-green-card',
    description: 'Self-sponsored concurrent filing framing for employer-dependent workers.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The EB-5 Investment Process',
    url: '/eb5-investment-process',
    description: 'Step-by-step filing sequence from eligibility through I-829.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayHero('eb5-for-l1');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway for L-1 visa holders"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 for L-1 Holders"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for L-1 Holders"
  keywords={['EB-5 for L-1', 'L-1A EB-5', 'L-1B green card', 'L-1 to EB-5']}
>
  <h2>L-1 status is employer-tied and time-limited</h2>
  <p>
    L-1A and L-1B classifications allow intracompany transfers, but they remain tied to a qualifying employer
    relationship and are subject to maximum stay limits. Extensions, role changes, and corporate reorganizations can
    affect eligibility. Professionals who want permanent residence that does not depend on continued transfer status
    sometimes evaluate EB-5 as a parallel or alternative track, without assuming any specific approval timeline.
  </p>

  <h2>Why employer independence matters for some L-1 holders</h2>
  <p>
    With EB-5, the investor is typically the petitioner for their own immigrant petition, rather than relying on the
    same employer that sponsors the L-1. That structure can matter for people who expect career changes, overseas
    rotations, or company risk. It does not remove the need for a qualifying investment, lawful source of funds, or
    compliant project. For a related employer-independence discussion aimed at H-1B households, see
    <a href="/pathways/h1b-to-green-card">H-1B to green card via EB-5</a>. For filing steps, see the
    <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Planning while L-1 remains valid</h2>
  <p>
    Some L-1 holders begin source-of-funds and project diligence while still in valid L status so that a future I-526E
    filing is not compressed against a maximum-stay deadline. Concurrent filing may be available for eligible investors
    already in the United States when visa numbers are current in the relevant category. Confirm that with counsel for
    your facts. EB-5 does not automatically extend L-1 status on its own.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
